with its query q(i). The inner product (q(i))ᵀk(i') measures how well the address i' matches the query, the softmax function turns the match scores into retrieval weights, and the output z(i) is the retrieved content. Because q(i) and k(i') are obtained from the different matrices WQ and WK, the attention weights are \emph{directed}: in general, αi,i' ≠ αi',i, so that token i attending strongly to token i' does not imply the reverse. Directionality arises because asking for information (the query) and advertising it (the key) are distinct roles. A classical dictionary data structure or hash table returns the single value stored at an exactly matching address . Attention instead returns a weighted average. Moreover, its output z(i) is a differentiable function of the matrices WQ, WK, and WV. In particular, the queries, keys, and values can themselves be learned via gradient-based methods for solving empirical risk minimization. The same retrieval principle, softened into a weighted average, underlies k-nearest neighbors methods. It also guides efficient implementations: locality-sensitive hashing restricts each query to nearby keys, reducing the cost of one attention head from n2 query--key comparisons to approximately n log n, with n again the number of tokens . Fig.~2 visualizes the attention weights αi,i' that a single head learns on sentences from the Universal Declaration of Human Rights . Trained to reconstruct each masked token from the others, the head gives many query tokens a weight concentrated on a few keys rather than spread uniformly, illustrating the associative-memory reading on this small corpus. The heat map also shows the directed nature of the weights discussed above: the query token ``rights'' places its full weight on the key token ``beings'' (α12,3 = 1.0), while the query token ``beings'' places no weight on the key token ``rights'' (α3,12 = 0.0).

A weather station at Krems an der Donau records eight measurements on each day of a year: the lowest, highest and mean air temperature, the precipitation, the sunshine duration, the humidity, the air pressure and the wind speed. Writing the eight measurements of one day as a row, and placing the rows of the 366 days under one another, gives a table of numbers. That table is the feature matrix of the dataset. Consider a dataset D of m data points with feature vectors x(1), …, x(m), each in Rd. It is convenient to collect the individual feature vectors into the feature matrix:  eaturemtx \defeq  ig( eaturevec^{(1)}, \,\ldots, \, eaturevec^{(\samplesize)} ig)^{	op} =  egin{pmatrix}  eature^{(1)}_{1} &  eature^{(1)}_{2} & \cdots &  eature^{(1)}_{
rfeatures}  eature^{(2)}_{1} &  eature^{(2)}_{2} & \cdots &  eature^{(2)}_{
rfeatures} \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots  eature^{(\samplesize)}_{1} &  eature^{(\samplesize)}_{2} & \cdots &  eature^{(\samplesize)}_{
rfeatures} \end{pmatrix} \in eals^{\samplesize 	imes 
rfeatures} 	ext{.} Note that the feature matrix is of size m × d, i.e., it has m rows and d columns. Each row holds one data point and each column holds one feature, so reading the feature matrix along a row or along a column answers two different questions (see Fig.~1). The feature matrix holds m d numbers, and a few summaries computed from it already say much about the learning task. The first is its shape, the pair (m, d). It says whether there are more data points than features. linear regression has a unique solution only when X has full column rank, which requires m ≥ d (see normal equations). More features than data points is the regime in which overfitting is unavoidable without regularization . The next summaries come from the singular value decomposition of X. Its singular values 1 ≥ … ≥ minm, d ≥ 0 are at most minm, d numbers, and the count of the nonzero ones is the rank of X . A rank below d means that some feature is a linear combination of the others and carries nothing new. The squares of the singular values are the eigenvalues of Xᵀ X, and this matrix divided by m is the sample covariance matrix of features with zero sample mean. The spectrum is therefore what principal component analysis uses to decide how many features a dimensionality reduction can keep. One number summarizes how far apart the singular values lie, the condition number X = 1 / rank(X). It is the ratio of the largest singular value to the smallest nonzero one. It bounds how far the linear regression solution moves when the labels are perturbed, and it governs how fast gradient descent converges on the least squares objective function . Fig.~2 shows these summaries for the weather year at Krems an der Donau. Each data point is a day and carries the eight measurements as its features, so the feature matrix is of shape (366, 8) and holds 2928 numbers. Each feature is first scaled to zero sample mean and unit sample variance, because the measurements carry different units. Eight singular values then summarize the whole year: the largest is 36.4 and the smallest 0.05, the two largest carry 64 percent of the sum of the squared singular values, and the four largest carry 89 percent. The smallest singular value lies far below the others, and it names a redundancy among the features. The mean temperature of a day differs from the midpoint of its lowest and its highest temperature by 0.02 degrees Celsius on average. Dropping the mean temperature raises the smallest singular value to 4.19 and lowers the condition number from 748 to 7.6. The rank is 8 either way, so the rank alone does not see this near-dependency and the singular values do.

Consider daily weather at Krems an der Donau. A linear model that predicts the maximum daytime temperature from the morning minimum is trained on 360 days of one year. The task-specific datasets are the temperature measurements of a single month, and one task is to learn a forecaster tailored to that month. For a single month, thirty gradient descent steps on that month's 30 days adjust the model parameters, starting from those learned on the whole year (Fig.~1). Fine-tuning is this second step: it continues the training of a model on a task-specific dataset, starting from model parameters obtained by pretraining on a larger dataset . Formally, let f(w) denote the average loss on the task-specific training set D(train) = z(r)r=1m, \[ f(\weights) \defeq  rac{1}{\samplesize} \sum_{\sampleidx=1}^{\samplesize} \lossfunc{\datapoint^{(\sampleidx)}}{\weights} 	ext{.} \] Fine-tuning solves the empirical risk minimization problem for f under the constraint that the model parameters stay within a radius B of the pretrained ones w(pre), \[ \widehat{\weights} \in \argmin_{\weights \in \closedball{\widehat{\weights}^{(\mathrm{pre})}}{B}} f(\weights) 	ext{.} \] Here, w(pre)B := w : w - w(pre) ≤ B is the closed ball of radius B around w(pre) (see Fig.~3). The radius B is what makes a small training set enough. Consider a linear model, whose hypotheses are h(w)(x) = wᵀ x. Let the feature vectors have norm at most R, and let the loss be ρ-Lipschitz in the prediction and bounded by c on the ball, as the hinge loss is. Let the data points be realizations of independent and identically distributed RVs. Then, with probability at least 1 - δ, every w ∈ w(pre)B satisfies \[ isk{\hypothesis^{(\weights)}} \leq f(\weights) +  rac{2 ho B R}{\sqrt{\samplesize}} + c \sqrt{ rac{2 \ln(2/\delta)}{\samplesize}} 	ext{.} \] This is the bound of for model parameters of norm at most B, applied to the shifted model parameters w - w(pre). The shift adds the fixed number (w(pre))ᵀ x to the prediction for each data point, which the contraction step of the proof allows, as it admits a separate ρ-Lipschitz function per data point . The second term follows from the Rademacher complexity of the ball, which is at most B R / √(m) . The generalization gap between risk and training error f(w) therefore grows in proportion to the radius B and not with the number d of model parameters. A gap of at most ε is guaranteed by m = (2 ρ B R + c √(2 ln(2/δ)))2 / ε2 data points, a number that falls with B. The constraint restricts the model the method searches, which is what regularization achieves by pruning a hypothesis space. The difference is that the pruned set is centered at the pretrained model parameters instead of at an arbitrary initialization. gradient descent with few steps and a small step size, started at the pretrained model parameters, implements this constraint without enforcing it explicitly. Each step moves the model parameters by η times the norm of the gradient. After T steps with step size η, started at w(0) = w(pre), \[ 
orm{\weights^{(
riter)} - \widehat{\weights}^{(\mathrm{pre})}} \leq \lrate \sum_{\iteridx=0}^{
riter-1} 
