riter G 	ext{,} \] where G bounds the norm of the gradient along the iterates. The iterate therefore lies in the ball of radius B = η T G around w(pre). Limiting the number of iterations is thus a form of regularization: it limits the effective hypothesis space that is searched during fine-tuning, with the step size and the iteration count in the role of the radius. Fine-tuning is useful when the task-specific dataset is small. A hospital has a few hundred annotated scans and needs a tumor detector. Training a deep net from an arbitrary initialization on that many labeled data points usually ends in overfitting; starting from model parameters already learned from millions of unrelated photographs works markedly better . LLMs are adapted the same way: one pretrained on unlabeled text is fine-tuned on each task it is wanted for . The experiment 	exttt{pythondemos/finetuning.py} measures the effect for linear regression with m = 15 data points and d = 30 model parameters. gradient descent started at the pretrained model parameters with step size η = 0.02 dips to a validation error below twice the noise level after T = 16 steps. It has then moved the model parameters a distance 0.32, inside the ball of radius η T G = 0.72. The same iteration from an arbitrary initialization drives the training error to zero yet ends with a validation error more than four times larger, having moved a distance 2.6. A common variant freezes some layers and adapts only the remainder ; parameter-efficient fine-tuning restricts the update to a low-dimensional set of new model parameters while leaving w(pre) untouched . Fig.~2 shows the effect on a small task-specific dataset that consists of four data points.

Fig.~1 shows a photograph of a mountain that is cut into small square patches, and each patch is described by two numbers: how green and how blue it is on average. Vegetation, mountain and haze, and bright sky form three clusters of such patches, which are data points characterized by a feature vector x ∈ Rd. A GMM is a probabilistic model for the generation of such data points. The idea of a GMM is to assume that each data point is generated by first drawing a latent cluster index I ∈ 1, …, k according to cluster probabilities \prob{I=\clusteridx} = p_{\clusteridx}, \qquad \sum_{\clusteridx=1}^{
rcluster} p_{\clusteridx}=1	ext{.} Given the choice I=c, the feature vector x is drawn from a multivariate normal distribution P(c)= cc, with mean c and covariance matrix c. The resulting marginal distribution of x is therefore a weighted sum of multivariate normal distributions (Fig.~1), i.e., \probdist =\sum_{\clusteridx=1}^{
rcluster} p_{\clusteridx} \mvnormal{\meanvec{\clusteridx}}{\covmtx{\clusteridx}}	ext{.} A GMM is parameterized by the cluster-specific probability pc = I=c, mean c, and covariance matrix c for c=1, …, k. These model parameters can be learned from a dataset D = x(1), …, x(m) by maximum likelihood. The resulting optimization problem has no closed-form solution, and the standard method for solving it approximately is the expectation–maximization algorithm , which alternates between two steps. The E-step computes, for every data point x(r) and every cluster index c, the posterior distribution under the current model parameters,  egin{equation} \dob{\clusteridx}{\sampleidx} \defeq \prob{I = \clusteridx \mid  eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)}} =  rac{p_{\clusteridx}\, \mvnormal{\meanvec{\clusteridx}}{\covmtx{\clusteridx}}( eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)})} {\sum_{\clusteridx'=1}^{
rcluster} p_{\clusteridx'}\, \mvnormal{\meanvec{\clusteridx'}}{\covmtx{\clusteridx'}}( eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)})}	ext{,} \end{equation} and the M-step re-fits the model parameters as averages weighted by these posterior distributions,  egin{equation}  egin{aligned} p_{\clusteridx} &\defeq  rac{1}{\samplesize} \sum_{\sampleidx=1}^{\samplesize} \dob{\clusteridx}{\sampleidx}, \qquad \meanvec{\clusteridx} \defeq  rac{\sum_{\sampleidx=1}^{\samplesize} \dob{\clusteridx}{\sampleidx}\,  eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)}} {\sum_{\sampleidx=1}^{\samplesize} \dob{\clusteridx}{\sampleidx}}, \covmtx{\clusteridx} &\defeq  rac{\sum_{\sampleidx=1}^{\samplesize} \dob{\clusteridx}{\sampleidx}\, ( eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)} - \meanvec{\clusteridx}) ( eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)} - \meanvec{\clusteridx})^{	op}} {\sum_{\sampleidx=1}^{\samplesize} \dob{\clusteridx}{\sampleidx}} \quad 	ext{for } \clusteridx = 1, \ldots, 
rcluster	ext{.} \end{aligned} \end{equation} The two steps are repeated until the model parameters stop changing. Trained by expectation–maximization on the 972 patches of Fig.~1, a three-component GMM separates vegetation, mountain and haze, and bright sky. Every patch it assigns to the bluest component lies in the upper half of the photograph. The learned model parameters define a soft clustering: the posterior distribution I = c | x is the probability that a data point belongs to cluster c, and it provides a principled way to quantify the degree of belonging to cluster c. In contrast, hard clustering methods assign each data point to exactly one cluster. Fig.~2 shows these degrees of belonging on the photograph itself. The maximum likelihood optimization problem for the model parameters of a GMM is an instance of empirical risk minimization with a particular choice for the loss function. Assume that every covariance matrix c is invertible, so that the GMM has a probability density function on Rd. The loss incurred by a feature vector x is the negative logarithm of this probability density function at x, \lossfunc{ eaturevec}{\probdist} = - \log \sum_{\clusteridx=1}^{
rcluster} p_{\clusteridx} \mvnormal{\meanvec{\clusteridx}}{\covmtx{\clusteridx}} ( eaturevec) 	ext{.} The empirical risk of a GMM is then obtained as the average loss over the entire dataset. Minimizing it maximizes the likelihood function, since the logarithm is increasing and the sign is flipped. Fitting a GMM is therefore empirical risk minimization over the hypothesis space of mixtures of k multivariate normal distributions. The loss function is a non-convex function of the model parameters, so no closed form solves the optimization problem . The expectation–maximization algorithm is an approximate method for the maximum likelihood optimization problem: it never increases the empirical risk. Since the expectation–maximization algorithm might converge to a local minimum of the empirical risk , it is typically run several times with different initializations, keeping the run with the smallest empirical risk . A common construction of the initial model parameters runs k-means first and starts from its cluster centroids . Like k-means, the expectation–maximization algorithm is a fixed-point iteration. Collect the model parameters of a GMM in one vector w. One iteration, the E-step \eqref{equ_gmm_estep_dict} followed by the M-step \eqref{equ_gmm_mstep_dict}, applies an operator F to the current model parameters, so the method is the fixed-point iteration w(t+1) = F(w(t)). The fixed points of F are model parameters at which the gradient of the empirical risk vanishes, i.e., the stationary points of the empirical risk (see zero-gradient condition) . convergence follows from monotone descent rather than from a contractive operator property: applying F never increases the empirical risk , so the sequence of empirical risk values converges, and under mild continuity conditions the iterates w(t) approach a fixed point . On the patches of Fig.~1, the empirical risk never increased in any of the 300 iterations of expectation–maximization. The expectation–maximization algorithm for learning the model parameters of a GMM reduces to Lloyd's algorithm for k-means under additional constraints. Fix every cluster probability at 1/k and every covariance matrix at σ2 I, a multiple of the identity matrix with one common σ2. The posterior distribution then depends on x only through its Euclidean distances to the means, and as σ2 shrinks towards 0 it concentrates on the closest one. In the limit (σ2 → 0), the E-step \eqref{equ_gmm_estep_dict} becomes an assignment of every data point to its nearest mean, and the M-step \eqref{equ_gmm_mstep_dict} re-centers the means on the data points assigned to them. That is Lloyd's algorithm for k-means. Fig.~3 shows the effect of the common σ2 on the degree of belonging to the vegetation cluster. With σ2 = 10-4, every patch obtains a degree of belonging of 0 or 1, and the grading coincides with the k-means assignment on more than 99% of the patches. With σ2 = 2 · 10-2, 63% of the patches obtain a degree of belonging between 0.2 and 0.8. On the patches of Fig.~1, with σ2 = 1 the three means collapse onto the average of all patches and every patch obtains the same degree of belonging 1/3. Leaving the covariance matrices and the cluster probabilities free is what lets a GMM provide a degree of belonging where k-means assigns each data point to one cluster.

Fig.~1 shows a photograph of a mountain that is cut into small square patches, and each patch is described by two numbers: how green and how blue it is on average. Vegetation, mountain and haze, and bright sky form three clusters of such patches, which are data points characterized by a feature vector x ∈ Rd. k-means is a hard clustering method for such data points. The idea of k-means is to assign each data point of a dataset D = x(1), …, x(m) to exactly one of k clusters indexed by c = 1, …, k. Each cluster is represented by a cluster centroid c ∈ Rd, and a data point is assigned to the cluster whose cluster centroid is nearest in Euclidean distance. The quality of a given choice of cluster centroids, and of the resulting hard clustering, is measured by the clustering error: the average squared Euclidean distance between a data point and its nearest cluster centroid,  rac{1}{\samplesize}\sum_{\sampleidx=1}^{\samplesize} \min_{\clusteridx = 1, \ldots, 
rcluster} 
ormgeneric{ eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)} - \clustercentroid{\clusteridx}}{2}^{2}	ext{.} The k-means principle is to choose cluster centroids that minimize the clustering error, \min_{\clustercentroid{1}, \ldots, \clustercentroid{
rcluster}} \; rac{1}{\samplesize}\sum_{\sampleidx=1}^{\samplesize} \min_{\clusteridx = 1, \ldots, 
rcluster} 
ormgeneric{ eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)} - \clustercentroid{\clusteridx}}{2}^{2}	ext{.} The inner minimization assigns each data point to its nearest cluster centroid, and the outer minimization moves the cluster centroids (Fig.~1). The clustering model of k-means is parameterized by the cluster centroids 1, …, k ∈ Rd. These model parameters are learned from a dataset by minimizing the clustering error. The resulting optimization problem has no closed-form solution, and the standard method for solving it approximately is Lloyd's algorithm , which alternates between two steps. The assignment step assigns every data point to its nearest cluster centroid,  egin{equation} \widehat{\clusteridx}^{(\sampleidx)} \defeq \argmin_{\clusteridx = 1, \ldots, 
rcluster} 
ormgeneric{ eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)} - \clustercentroid{\clusteridx}}{2} \quad 	ext{for } \sampleidx = 1, \ldots, \samplesize	ext{,} \end{equation} and the averaging step moves each cluster centroid to the mean of the data points assigned to it,  egin{equation} \clustercentroid{\clusteridx} \defeq  rac{1}{ ig|\{\sampleidx : \widehat{\clusteridx}^{(\sampleidx)} = \clusteridx\} ig|} \sum_{\sampleidx : \widehat{\clusteridx}^{(\sampleidx)} = \clusteridx}  eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)} \quad 	ext{for } \clusteridx = 1, \ldots, 
rcluster	ext{.} \end{equation} The two steps are repeated until the assignment \eqref{equ_kmeans_assign_dict} stops changing. Algorithm~1 states the method in full.  egin{algorithm}[H] \caption{k-means, Lloyd's algorithm }  egin{algorithmic}[1] \Require feature vectors x(1), …, x(m), cluster count k, initial cluster centroids 1, …, k \Repeat \State 	extbf{assignment:} for each r = 1, …, m, assign x(r) to the cluster argminc = 1, …, k x(r) - c2, as in \eqref{equ_kmeans_assign_dict} \State 	extbf{averaging:} for each c = 1, …, k, set c to the mean of the feature vectors assigned to cluster c, as in \eqref{equ_kmeans_average_dict} \Until{no assignment changes} \Ensure cluster centroids 1, …, k and the assignment they induce \end{algorithmic} \end{algorithm} Run on the 972 patches of Fig.~1 with k = 3, k-means separates vegetation, mountain and haze, and bright sky. Every patch it assigns to the bluest cluster centroid lies in the upper half of the photograph. The learned cluster centroids define a hard clustering: every data point belongs to exactly one cluster, the one with the nearest cluster centroid. In contrast, soft clustering methods assign the same data point to several clusters, with varying degrees of belonging. Fig.~2 shows this assignment on the photograph itself. The k-means principle is an instance of empirical risk minimization with a particular choice for the hypothesis space and the loss function. The hypothesis space Hk consists of all maps h: Rd → Rd of the piecewise-constant form \hypothesis( eaturevec) = \clustercentroid{\,\clusteridx^{\star}( eaturevec)}, \qquad \clusteridx^{\star}( eaturevec) = \argmin_{\clusteridx = 1, \ldots, 
rcluster} 
ormgeneric{ eaturevec - \clustercentroid{\clusteridx}}{2}	ext{,} parameterized by the k cluster centroids 1, …, k ∈ Rd. Each such hypothesis maps every feature vector to its nearest cluster centroid. The region of feature space mapped to one cluster centroid is bounded by hyperplanes, one for each other cluster centroid (the dashed lines of Fig.~1). Thus, every cluster found by k-means is a convex region. The loss incurred by a feature vector x is the squared Euclidean distance to its nearest cluster centroid, xh = x - h(x)22. The clustering error is then obtained as the average loss, i.e., the empirical risk, over the entire dataset. Minimizing the clustering error is therefore empirical risk minimization over Hk. The clustering error is a non-convex function of the cluster centroids: with a single data point at x = 0 and two scalar cluster centroids w(1), w(2) ∈ R, it is min((w(1))2, (w(2))2), so both (1, 0) and (0, 1) are global optima with value 0, but their midpoint (0.5, 0.5) gives 0.25. Solving the optimization problem exactly is NP-hard . Lloyd's algorithm is an approximate method for the k-means optimization problem: it never increases the clustering error. Since Lloyd's algorithm might converge to a local minimum of the clustering error, it is typically run several times with different initializations, keeping the run with the smallest clustering error . A variant of k-means referred to as k-means++ uses a specific construction of the initial cluster centroids. Like the expectation–maximization algorithm for a Gaussian mixture model, Lloyd's algorithm is a fixed-point iteration. Collect the cluster centroids in one vector w := (1, …, k). One iteration, the assignment step \eqref{equ_kmeans_assign_dict} followed by the averaging step \eqref{equ_kmeans_average_dict}, applies an operator F to the current cluster centroids, so the method is the fixed-point iteration w(t+1) = F(w(t)). The fixed points of F are cluster centroids that each coincide with the mean of the data points assigned to them. convergence follows from monotone descent rather than from a contractive operator property: each of the two steps minimizes the clustering error over one set of variables with the other held fixed, so applying F never increases the clustering error. A finite dataset admits only finitely many partitions, so the iterates reach a fixed point after finitely many iterations . On the patches of Fig.~1, Lloyd's algorithm reached a fixed point after 12 iterations, and the clustering error never increased along the way. Lloyd's algorithm for k-means is the limit of the expectation–maximization algorithm for a Gaussian mixture model under additional constraints. Fix every cluster probability of the Gaussian mixture model at 1/k and every covariance matrix at σ2 I, a multiple of the identity matrix with one common σ2. The posterior distribution of the cluster index then depends on x only through its Euclidean distances to the means, and as σ2 shrinks towards 0 it concentrates on the closest one. In the limit (σ2 → 0), the E-step of expectation–maximization becomes the assignment step \eqref{equ_kmeans_assign_dict} and the M-step becomes the averaging step \eqref{equ_kmeans_average_dict} of Lloyd's algorithm. On the patches of Fig.~1 with σ2 = 10-4, the expectation–maximization algorithm assigns 99.4% of the patches like k-means. Leaving the covariance matrices and the cluster probabilities free is what lets a Gaussian mixture model provide a degree of belonging where k-means assigns each data point to one cluster. The loss function is what separates k-means from its generalizations, and it decides what the averaging step of Lloyd's algorithm computes. Replacing the squared Euclidean distance by the Euclidean distance itself, xh = x - h(x)2, gives the k-medians optimization problem. The assignment step \eqref{equ_kmeans_assign_dict} is unchanged, since the nearest cluster centroid is the same under both losses, but the averaging step \eqref{equ_kmeans_average_dict} becomes the geometric median of the data points assigned to a cluster, which has no closed form and is computed by Weiszfeld's fixed-point iteration . The gain is robustness to outliers: adding 10 far-away patches to the 566 patches of the vegetation cluster of Fig.~1 moves their mean by 0.044 but their geometric median by 0.004, while k-medians assigns 98.5% of the patches like k-means. The averaging step is not tied to the squared Euclidean distance alone: for any Bregman divergence xh = xh(x)φ as loss, the mean of the assigned data points still minimizes their summed loss, so only the assignment step changes . The Euclidean distance is not a Bregman divergence, which is why it changes the averaging step. Restricting each cluster centroid to be one of the data points assigned to it gives k-medoids, which uses only the pairwise distances between data points and therefore applies to data points without numeric feature vectors . Fig.~3 shows the effect of the number k of clusters on the patches of Fig.~1. Each patch is painted with the average color of its cluster, which is image segmentation. With k = 2, the sky and the mountain are separated from the vegetation. With k = 3, the mountain and haze form their own cluster. With k = 4, the vegetation splits into the sunlit meadow and the dark foliage. The clustering error decreases with k, from 0.034 for k = 2 to 0.018 for k = 3 and 0.011 for k = 4, and it reaches 0 when every data point is its own cluster centroid.

Consider an iterative machine learning method for learning a useful hypothesis h ∈ H. Such a method repeats similar update steps that modify the current hypothesis to obtain an improved one. A learning rate is a parameter of such a method that controls the extent to which the current hypothesis can be modified during a single iteration. A prime example for a learning rate is the step size η of gradient descent ,  egin{equation} \weights^{(\iteridx\!+\!1)} = \weights^{(\iteridx)} - \lrate 
abla f(\weights^{(\iteridx)}) 	ext{.} \end{equation} Here, the objective function f(w) is the empirical risk incurred by a hypothesis h(w) on a training set, and each gradient step moves the model parameters against its gradient ∇ f(w(t)) by a distance proportional to η. Fig.~1 illustrates one gradient step \eqref{equ_def_basic_gradstep_lrate_dict} of gradient descent for a linear model, started from the same hypothesis with three choices of the learning rate. The learning rates are given as multiples of 1/L, with L the largest eigenvalue of the Hessian of f. For the squared error loss, a gradient step lowers f for every learning rate below 2/L ; the largest learning rate of Fig.~1 lies above that threshold, and its step raises f. Each iteration of an machine learning method should make large progress towards a useful hypothesis, so the learning rate should not be too small. However, it can also be too large: if the step size of gradient descent is too large, the gradient steps can overshoot a minimum of the objective function. Fig.~2 illustrates both cases. Consider an iterative machine learning method that learns a hypothesis from some underlying hypothesis space H by running a fixed number T of iterations. Varying the learning rate can be used to implement regularization via effectively pruning H. For example, each gradient step above \eqref{equ_def_basic_gradstep_lrate_dict} moves the model parameters by η ∇ f(w(t)). By the triangle inequality,  egin{equation} 
orm{\weights^{(
riter)} - \weights^{(0)}} \leq \lrate \sum_{\iteridx=0}^{
riter-1} 
orm{
abla f(\weights^{(\iteridx)})} 	ext{.} \end{equation} Together with T, the learning rate thus determines the region of the parameter space that the method can reach, and thereby the effective hypothesis space: the hypotheses h(w) whose model parameters w lie in that region. Fig.~3 draws the balls around w(0) with the radius \eqref{equ_learnrate_reach_dict} for T = 10 gradient steps and three learning rates. Reducing the learning rate shrinks the effective hypothesis space that can be reached by the gradient steps. Choosing the learning rate too small or too large can then result in underfitting or overfitting. As a hyperparameter, the learning rate can be chosen by validation: the hypotheses learned with different learning rates are compared by their validation error on a validation set.

Linear regression methods learn a linear hypothesis map that delivers a prediction of the numeric label of a data point. The prediction is based solely on the features of the data point, which are fed as input to the learned hypothesis map. Linear regression can be used to predict tomorrow's temperature from weather measurements recorded over the last five days, using them as features and tomorrow's temperature as the label. Formally, linear regression learns a linear hypothesis map h(w)(x) = wᵀx to predict the numeric label y ∈ R of a data point from its feature vector x = (x1, …, xd)ᵀ ∈ Rd. Here, d denotes the number of features of a data point. The model parameters w are learned from a training set D(train) = x(r)y(r) r=1m of data points. Appending a constant feature to x lets one entry of w act as an intercept, so wᵀx represents an affine function of the original measurements. In what follows, x denotes the feature vector that is fed to the linear hypothesis map. It can be either the original or the augmented feature vector. The least squares variant of linear regression measures the quality of a linear hypothesis map by the average squared error loss on the training set. As an instance of empirical risk minimization, it learns the model parameters w by solving the optimization problem \[ \min_{\weights \in eals^{
rfeatures}}  rac{1}{\samplesize} \sum_{\sampleidx=1}^{\samplesize}  ig( 	ruelabel^{(\sampleidx)} - \weights^{	op}  eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)}  ig)^{2} 	ext{.} \] Fig.~1 illustrates this linear regression problem for a training set with a constant scalar feature x(r)=1 for r=1,…,m. The optimization problem can be written more compactly using the feature matrix X and the label vector y, \[  eaturemtx =  ig( eaturevec^{(1)},\,\ldots,\, eaturevec^{(\samplesize)} ig)^{	op} \in eals^{\samplesize 	imes 
rfeatures} 	ext{,} \qquad \labelvec =  ig( 	ruelabel^{(1)},\,\ldots,\,	ruelabel^{(\samplesize)}  ig)^{	op} \in eals^{\samplesize} 	ext{.} \] In terms of X and y, the optimization problem reads \[ \min_{\weights \in eals^{
rfeatures}} \underbrace{ rac{1}{\samplesize} 
ormgeneric{\labelvec -  eaturemtx \weights}{2}^{2}}_{f(\weights)} 	ext{.} \] The average squared error loss f(w) is a convex and differentiable function of w. By the zero-gradient condition, a vector w solves this optimization problem if and only if it satisfies the system of linear equations  egin{equation}  eaturemtx^{	op} eaturemtx \widehat{\weights} =  eaturemtx^{	op} \labelvec 	ext{.} \end{equation} The normal equations \eqref{eq_linreg_normal_eq_dict} always have a solution because Xᵀy lies in the column space of XᵀX. However, the solution of the normal equations \eqref{eq_linreg_normal_eq_dict} is unique only if the feature matrix X has full column rank. This requires at least as many data points as features, m ≥ d. When this holds, and the columns of X are linearly independent, the matrix XᵀX is invertible and the normal equations \eqref{eq_linreg_normal_eq_dict} have the unique closed-form solution w = (XᵀX)-1 Xᵀ y. If instead m < d (more features than data points), X cannot have full column rank, XᵀX is singular, and the optimization problem has infinitely many solutions. All of them incur the same minimal average squared error loss on the training set, but their predictions for data points outside the training set can differ arbitrarily. Choosing among them is therefore a matter of generalization (see overfitting). A unique solution can be selected, for example, by picking the minimum-norm solution X y given by the pseudoinverse X. A common remedy is regularization: adding a penalty term to the average squared error loss. The penalty term can be interpreted as an estimate of how much higher the loss is on data points outside the training set than on it . This yields regularized variants of linear regression, with ridge regression using the penalty term α w22 and the least absolute shrinkage and selection operator using the penalty term α w1, where α > 0 is a hyperparameter that sets the weight of the penalty term. Linear regression also has a statistical interpretation. Consider a probabilistic model with a joint probability distribution P(x, y) over the features and the label. Under the squared error loss, the Bayes estimator (i.e., the hypothesis with minimum risk) is the conditional expectation Ey | x. In this statistical interpretation, x denotes the original feature vector. Let the features and the label be jointly Gaussian RVs with zero mean and invertible covariance matrix x = Exxᵀ. This Bayes estimator is then \[  ayeshypothesis( eaturevec) =  ig(\weights^{\star} ig)^{	op} eaturevec 	ext{, with } \weights^{\star} =  ig(\covmtx{ eaturevec} ig)^{-1}\, \covvec{ eaturevec,	ruelabel} 	ext{.} \] Here, x,y = E is the covariance between the features x and the label y. This Bayes estimator is linear in x. Without the zero-mean assumption, it is an affine function of x. However, as for the linear hypothesis map above, the intercept can be absorbed by appending a constant feature. Least-squares linear regression is the empirical risk minimization counterpart of this predictor. It replaces x and x,y with the sample-based estimates (1/m)XᵀX and (1/m)Xᵀy. For invertible XᵀX, this recovers the unique solution w = (XᵀX)-1Xᵀy of the normal equations \eqref{eq_linreg_normal_eq_dict}. Instead of solving the normal equations \eqref{eq_linreg_normal_eq_dict} directly (via the inverse matrix or pseudoinverse), machine learning methods often solve it by an iterative optimization method, as implemented in widely used machine learning software libraries such as scikit-learn and PyTorch. Starting from initial model parameters w(0), such a method repeatedly applies an update operator F, \[ \weights^{(\iteridx+1)} =  ixedpointop ig(\weights^{(\iteridx)} ig) 	ext{, for } \iteridx = 0,1,\ldots 	ext{.} \] The operator F is designed such that the sequence of model parameters w(t) converges to a solution of the normal equations \eqref{eq_linreg_normal_eq_dict}. One important example of such an iterative optimization method is gradient descent, which uses the gradient descent step operator \[ \gdstep{\lrate}(\weights) = \weights - \lrate 
abla f(\weights) 	ext{.} \] Here, η > 0 is a step size that must be chosen small enough to ensure convergence. The superscript in η makes the dependence of the operator on η explicit. Inserting the average training error into the general form of the gradient descent step yields the explicit update \[  egin{aligned} \gdstep{\lrate}(\weights) &= \weights +  rac{2\lrate}{\samplesize} \sum_{\sampleidx=1}^{\samplesize}  ig( 	ruelabel^{(\sampleidx)} - \weights^{	op} eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)}  ig)  eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)} &= \Big(\mI -  rac{2\lrate}{\samplesize} eaturemtx^{	op} eaturemtx\Big)\weights +  rac{2\lrate}{\samplesize} eaturemtx^{	op}\labelvec 	ext{.} \end{aligned} \] The fixed points of η are exactly the solutions of the normal equations \eqref{eq_linreg_normal_eq_dict}, since η(w) = w holds if and only if XᵀXw = Xᵀy. The update operator is affine, \gdstep{\lrate}(\weights) = \mM \weights + \vb 	ext{,} with linear part M = I - (2η/m)XᵀX and offset b = (2η/m) Xᵀy. When X has full column rank, so that XᵀX is positive definite, η is a contractive operator with respect to the Euclidean norm for every step size 0 < η < m/max. Here, max is the largest eigenvalue of XᵀX. The eigenvalues of the linear part M are 1 - (2η/m) j, with the positive eigenvalues j of XᵀX. They lie strictly between -1 and 1 exactly for such step sizes (cf.\ , which covers gradient descent for any smooth, strongly convex objective function and step sizes η ≤ m/(min + max), with the smallest eigenvalue min of XᵀX). The fixed-point iteration converges to the unique solution w. Moreover, the convergence speed of gradient descent is governed by the ratio of the largest to the smallest eigenvalue of XᵀX, that is, by its condition number XᵀX = max/min . The gradient descent step above uses the entire training set to compute the gradient. When the data points instead arrive sequentially at time instants t=1,2,…, or the training set is too large to fit in memory, the model parameters can be learned by online gradient descent. At each time step t, it applies a single gradient descent step. That step uses only the arriving data point x(t)y(t), \[ \weights^{(\iteridx+1)} = \weights^{(\iteridx)} + 2\lrate  ig( 	ruelabel^{(\iteridx)} -  ig(\weights^{(\iteridx)} ig)^{	op}  eaturevec^{(\iteridx)}  ig)  eaturevec^{(\iteridx)} 	ext{.} \] This is the gradient descent step on the loss function (y(t) - wᵀx(t))2 of the arriving data point alone, rather than on the average squared error loss f(w). It is the least mean squares update. It avoids storing the entire training set, and its per-iteration complexity is independent of the training set size m. The optimality condition \eqref{eq_linreg_normal_eq_dict} allows one to study the stability of linear regression. Ideally, the learned model parameters are insensitive to limited perturbations of the training set. A label-only perturbation, for example, replaces a single label of the training set with an outlier or another corrupted value. A perturbed training set yields a feature matrix X = X + Δ X and a label vector y = y + Δ y, with perturbation matrix Δ X and vector Δ y. This gives the perturbed normal equations  egin{equation} \widetilde{ eaturemtx}^{	op} \widetilde{ eaturemtx} \widetilde{\weights} = \widetilde{ eaturemtx}^{	op} \widetilde{\labelvec} 	ext{.} \end{equation} matrix perturbation theory quantifies how much w deviates from a solution w of the clean normal equations \eqref{eq_linreg_normal_eq_dict} . For a label-only perturbation, Δ X = 0, the perturbed and clean normal equations share the coefficient matrix XᵀX. Assume X has full column rank, so XᵀX is invertible and the solution is unique. Subtracting the clean normal equations \eqref{eq_linreg_normal_eq_dict} from the perturbed normal equations \eqref{eq_linreg_perturbed_normal_eq_dict} yields \[ \widetilde{\weights} - \widehat{\weights} =  ig( eaturemtx^{	op} eaturemtx ig)^{-1}  eaturemtx^{	op} \Delta\labelvec = \pinv{ eaturemtx} \Delta\labelvec 	ext{,} \] with the pseudoinverse X. The effect of perturbing the training set is thereby quantified, \[ 
ormgeneric{\widetilde{\weights} - \widehat{\weights}}{2} \leq 
ormgeneric{\pinv{ eaturemtx}}{2}\, 
ormgeneric{\Delta\labelvec}{2} 	ext{.} \] When X has full column rank, the pseudoinverse has the norm X2 = 1/min(XᵀX) = XᵀX/X2, where XᵀX = max/min is the condition number of XᵀX (see pseudoinverse). In the setting of Fig.~1, X = 1 (the all-ones vector), so X Δy = (1/m) Σr=1m Δy(r). Fig.~2 illustrates such a label-only perturbation of the training set from Fig.~1: the single label perturbation Δy(3) = 6 gives w - w = 6/3 = 2, i.e., the outlier pulls the average of the labels upward, from w = 3 to w = 5. The effect of perturbations can also be studied on the level of a specific optimization method. For example, the update of online gradient descent becomes \[  egin{aligned} \weights^{(\iteridx+1)} &= \weights^{(\iteridx)} + 2\lrate  ig( \widetilde{	ruelabel}^{(\iteridx)} -  ig(\weights^{(\iteridx)} ig)^{	op} \widetilde{ eaturevec}^{(\iteridx)}  ig) \widetilde{ eaturevec}^{(\iteridx)} &= \weights^{(\iteridx)} + 2\lrate  ig( 	ruelabel^{(\iteridx)} -  ig(\weights^{(\iteridx)} ig)^{	op}  eaturevec^{(\iteridx)}  ig)  eaturevec^{(\iteridx)} + \perturbation{\iteridx} 	ext{.} \end{aligned} \] Here, t is a perturbation term that depends on the data point perturbation Δ x(t)Δ y(t) and the current model parameters w(t).

A weather station reports one temperature reading per hour, and a forecast for the next hour must be issued before that reading arrives. An offline method such as linear regression first collects readings into a training set and trains a model on it once. It then forecasts with the learned model parameters for good: while it collects, it issues no forecast, and after it has trained, it no longer learns. An online learning method instead adapts its model parameters every time a new reading arrives; Fig.~1 shows both on three days of readings. Online learning proceeds in rounds: in each round, a machine learning method commits to model parameters, receives a feedback signal from its environment, and updates. The updates are based on some form of feedback. For the weather station, the feedback is the new data point itself. Any new temperature reading allows to compute the squared error loss against the current model parameters, and the loss can be evaluated for any possible choice of the model parameters. The feedback is therefore an entire loss function w ↦ z(t)w . online gradient descent is the online learning method for feedback by an entire loss function: it updates the model parameters by a gradient step on that loss function . Some online learning applications provide only a single loss or reward value as feedback (Fig.~2). For example, a clinic that gives one of several treatments observes the outcome of that treatment alone, which is a multiarmed bandit . A thermostat whose heating decision changes the temperature it faces in the next hour receives a reward together with a new state, which is reinforcement learning . Consider model parameters w from a convex closed set W ⊆ Rd, the parameter space, and rounds at consecutive time instants t = 1, 2, …. At time t, the environment fixes a function f(t): W → R, and the current model parameters w(t) incur the loss f(t)(w(t)). Only then is a feedback signal r(t) about f(t) revealed. Three kinds of feedback occur. With full information, the data point z(t) is revealed, and it determines f(t)(w) = z(t)w for every w ∈ W. On the temperature stream, the data point z(t) = (x(t), y(t)) has as features, collected in the feature vector x(t), the readings of the previous hours, and as label the reading of hour t. Its loss function f(t) is the squared error loss of the forecast h(w)(x(t)). With bandit feedback, only the value f(t)(w(t)) is revealed. In an multiarmed bandit with A actions, the model parameters w(t) ∈ A-1 form the probability mass function from which the action a(t) is drawn. The environment fixes a loss vector ℓ(t) ∈ [0,1]A, whose entry ℓ(t)a is the loss of action a in round t, so the expected loss of the draw is f(t)(w) = ℓ(t)w. The clinic observes the single entry ℓ(t)a(t), the loss of the action drawn, and no other entry. With reinforcement learning feedback, the immediate reward r(s(t), a(t)) of the action taken and the next state s(t+1) of a Markov decision process are revealed. The loss f(t)(w) is the negative expected reward of the policy with model parameters w in the state s(t). That state depends on the actions of the earlier rounds, so the environment's losses depend on the method's own past. The method then updates the model parameters using the feedback,  egin{equation} \weights^{(	imeidx+1)} \defeq  ixedpointop^{(	imeidx)} ig(\weights^{(	imeidx)}, eward^{(	imeidx)} ig) 	ext{,} \end{equation} with an update operator F(t) specific to the method, the counterpart of the operator F of a fixed-point iteration (Fig.~3). The feedback signal is written r(t), in the reward notation of reinforcement learning, although with full information it is a data point and with bandit feedback a loss value. The time index is also the iteration index: one update for any newly arriving data point. This contrasts with batch learning, which solves empirical risk minimization once over a fixed training set and then uses the learned hypothesis. The update \eqref{equ_def_onlinelearning_update_dict} at time t applies an operator that depends on the feedback signal r(t). By contrast, gradient descent applies a fixed gradient step operator in every time step. An online learning method is therefore not a fixed point iteration of one fixed operator. Instead, it concatenates operators that vary over the time steps. Unlike stochastic gradient descent, which uses one data point per step to approximate the gradient of one fixed objective function, an online learning method faces a different loss function in every round. With full information, online gradient descent uses the gradient g(t) := ∇ f(t)(w(t)) in a projected gradient step. online mirror descent replaces the Euclidean distance of the projection by a Bregman divergence, e.g., the Kullback–Leibler divergence on the probability simplex, which gives the Hedge update of the probability mass function over the actions . Follow-The-Regularized-Leader commits, in every round, to a minimizer of the accumulated loss of all rounds so far plus a regularizer . With bandit feedback, the gradient of f(t) is not available, since only one entry of ℓ(t) is observed. Exp3 forms an unbiased estimate of ℓ(t) by dividing the observed entry by the probability of the drawn action, and applies the online mirror descent update to the estimate . With reinforcement learning feedback, Q-learning changes one entry of a table of action values, the entry of the state visited and the action taken. The entry moves by a fixed fraction of the difference between its target, the reward received plus the discounted largest value of the next state, and its current value. This is a stochastic step of the Bellman operator, a contraction , so Q-learning, unlike online gradient descent, does have a fixed point interpretation . Policy gradient methods instead estimate the gradient of the expected reward from the rewards collected along the visited states . The figure of merit of an online learning method is not the decrease of an objective function along the iterates, which measures the progress of gradient descent: online learning has no fixed objective function. The loss function f(t) of round t is evaluated once, around w(t), and the next round brings a different loss function. Instead, an online learning method is evaluated by its regret. The regret is the excessive accumulated loss compared to that of a fixed choice w ∈ W of the model parameters. This fixed choice w is kept for all rounds t = 1, …, T and can be chosen after all losses are known. After T rounds, the regret relative to w ∈ W is \[ egret{
riter}{\weights} \defeq \sum_{	imeidx=1}^{
riter} f^{(	imeidx)}(\weights^{(	imeidx)}) - \sum_{	imeidx=1}^{
riter} f^{(	imeidx)}(\weights) 	ext{.} \] In an multiarmed bandit, the fixed w of interest is the probability mass function concentrated on one action. The regret is then measured against the best single action in hindsight; in reinforcement learning, against the best fixed policy . No assumption is made on how the losses are generated: the regret is defined for every sequence f(1), f(2), …, even one chosen by an opponent who has seen w(t). An online learning method is successful if its regret per round, Tw/T, vanishes as T grows for every w ∈ W . 	extbf{Regret bounds.} The rate at which the regret per round vanishes depends on the feedback. With full information and convex losses whose gradients have norm at most G on a parameter space of diameter D, online gradient descent with the learning rate η(t) = D/(G√(t)) has Tw 32 DG√(T) . No online algorithm can guarantee a regret below a constant times DG√(T) for every sequence: for each algorithm there is a sequence of losses on which its regret is at least that . With bandit feedback over A actions, the expected regret of Exp3 is at most 2√((e-1) T A log A) : observing one entry of ℓ(t) instead of all of them costs the factor √(A). With reinforcement learning feedback in an Markov decision process with S states and A actions, the UCRL2 method has regret at most a constant times S √(A T log T), the constant depending on how many steps it takes to reach any state from any other . In each case the regret per round vanishes as T grows. Fig.~4 shows the regret per round of online gradient descent and of follow-the-leader, which commits in every round to a minimizer of the accumulated loss so far, on a year of hourly temperature readings. For linear regression with the squared error loss, that minimizer is computed by the recursive least-squares update, which carries the least-squares solution from time t - 1 to time t in closed form . Fig.~5 turns the same stream into an multiarmed bandit. The actions are five fixed forecasters, each a rule that needs no training. They forecast the previous reading, the reading of the same hour one day earlier, the mean of the three previous readings, the linear extrapolation of the two previous readings, and the mean of all readings of the year so far. The loss of an hour is the scaled absolute error of the forecaster used, and the regret is measured against the best single forecaster in hindsight. With full information, all five errors are observed after each hour and Hedge updates the probability mass function; with bandit feedback, only the error of the forecaster used is observed and Exp3 updates the probability mass function from its estimate of the loss vector. The regret of Hedge after T rounds is at most √(2 T log A) for losses in [0,1], a weakening of the bound log A / η + η T / 8 of at the learning rate η = √(2 log A / T); the expected regret of Exp3 is at most 2√((e-1) T A log A) . Both regrets per round vanish, and the regret of Exp3 stays several times that of Hedge, the increase in regret caused by bandit feedback.

Consider an artificial intelligence system that uses logistic regression to learn an image classifier. logistic regression amounts to finding the minimum of a differentiable convex objective function, the empirical risk \[ f(\weights) \defeq  rac{1}{\samplesize} \sum_{\sampleidx=1}^{\samplesize} \lossfunc{\datapoint^{(\sampleidx)}}{\weights} 	ext{.} \] Here, z(r)w denotes the loss incurred on the data point z(r), a feature vector together with its label, of a training set D(train) = z(1), …, z(m) by the hypothesis with model parameters w. The number of data points in the training set is m. gradient descent searches for a minimum of a differentiable convex function by repeating the gradient step \[ \weights^{(\iteridx+1)} = \weights^{(\iteridx)} - \lrate^{(\iteridx)} 
abla f(\weights^{(\iteridx)}) 	ext{,} \] with a step size η(t) > 0 that is small enough for each step to decrease f (see gradient descent). The gradient of the above objective function is ∇ f(w) = (1)/(m) Σr=1m ∇w z(r)w, an average with one term per data point, so its exact evaluation requires a pass over the entire training set. SGD instead uses the estimate \[ g(\weights) \defeq  rac{1}{ atchsize} \sum_{\sampleidx \in  atch} 
abla_{\weights} \lossfunc{\datapoint^{(\sampleidx)}}{\weights} 	ext{,} \] the same average over a batch B ⊆ 1, …, m of B = |B| indices chosen uniformly at random (see Fig.~1). Each SGD iteration generates a new batch B(t) and performs the gradient descent update with the estimate in place of the gradient, \[ \weights^{(\iteridx+1)} = \weights^{(\iteridx)} - \lrate^{(\iteridx)}  rac{1}{ atchsize} \sum_{\sampleidx \in  atch^{(\iteridx)}} 
abla_{\weights} \lossfunc{\datapoint^{(\sampleidx)}}{\weights^{(\iteridx)}} 	ext{,} \] with a step size η(t) > 0. Only the gradient estimate differs from gradient descent (see Fig.~2). The update is a random instance of the gradient descent operator: for a uniformly drawn batch, its expectation is the gradient descent step w ↦ w - η(t) ∇ f(w), whose fixed points are the points satisfying the zero-gradient condition ∇ f(w) = 0. One classical set of sufficient conditions for the convergence of such random iterations comes from stochastic approximation : step sizes that diminish such that Σt=1∞ η(t) = ∞ while Σt=1∞ (η(t))2 < ∞ . The batch size B is an important parameter of SGD: B = m recovers gradient descent, while B = 1 updates with the gradient of a single data point and yields the noisiest estimate. SGD with 1 < B < m is mini-batch SGD . Each update costs B gradient evaluations instead of m. This is what makes empirical risk minimization on large training sets feasible, the main application of SGD in machine learning; the price is noise in the update, whose variance shrinks as the batch grows . When the training set is spread over several servers, as in Fig.~1, a batch can be drawn from one server at a time, so no server needs the whole training set.

The SVM is a binary classification method for data points with feature space X=Rd. In its simplest form, the SVM learns a linear classifier with decision boundary \{  eaturevec \in eals^{
rfeatures} : \weights^{	op}  eaturevec + \offset = 0 \}. The linear classifier is parameterized by a nonzero vector w ∈ Rd 0 and an offset b ∈ R. The vector w is the normal vector to the decision boundary and the offset b shifts the decision boundary away from the origin. For a data point with feature vector x and label y ∈ -1, +1, the SVM delivers the prediction y = sign(wᵀ x + b). SVMs have been applied to text classification, where each document is first represented by a numeric feature vector of word frequencies and then categorized by topic. Another application is handwritten digit recognition, where digit images are classified from pixel-level features . The SVM can be formulated as an instance of regularized empirical risk minimization for learning a linear classifier from a training set that consists of m data points. The model parameters (w, b) of the linear classifier are obtained as  egin{equation} (\widehat{\weights}, \widehat{\offset}) = \argmin_{\weights \in eals^{
rfeatures},\, \offset \in eals} \underbrace{ rac{1}{\samplesize} \sum_{\sampleidx=1}^{\samplesize} \max\{0,\, 1 - 	ruelabel^{(\sampleidx)} (\weights^{	op}  eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)} + \offset)\} + egparam 
ormgeneric{\weights}{2}^{2}}_{\defeq f(\weights, \offset)}	ext{.} \end{equation} The SVM objective function consists of the average hinge loss on the training set plus a penalty term α w22 with regularization parameter α > 0. The penalty term in the SVM problem \eqref{eq:svm_rerm} is the scaled squared Euclidean norm α w22, the same penalty term as in ridge regression. It does not involve the model parameter b, which is therefore left unpenalized. In linear regression, the penalty term can be interpreted as an estimate of how much higher the loss is on data points outside the training set than on it (see linear regression). This interpretation rests on the quadratic structure of the squared error loss and does not carry over to the hinge loss. For the SVM, the penalty term instead controls the generalization gap: the hinge loss is Lipschitz continuity continuous in w, with a constant given by the Euclidean norm of the feature vector. Adding the penalty term α w22 to the average hinge loss has three effects: First, it makes the overall objective function in the SVM problem \eqref{eq:svm_rerm} strongly convex in w, so that its solution w is unique. Second, it controls how much that solution varies if the data points in the training set change. Third, it keeps w2 small and therefore the margins wide . If the feature vectors in the training set have bounded Euclidean norm, the expectation of the difference between the risk and the empirical hinge loss of the learned linear classifier decreases in proportion to 1/(α m). The penalty term also has a geometric role: a small norm w2 corresponds to a large margin 1/w2 of the learned linear classifier, as discussed below. Like logistic regression and linear regression, the SVM learns the model parameters (w, b) of a linear model. In contrast to logistic regression and linear regression, the SVM objective function f(w, b) is non-smooth because of the hinge loss. However, since the SVM objective function is convex, the optimization problem \eqref{eq:svm_rerm} can be solved by convex optimization methods. One such method is subgradient descent, which is obtained from gradient descent by replacing the gradient of the objective function with a subgradient. Starting from an initial choice w(0), b(0) of the model parameters, subgradient descent repeatedly applies the update \[  ig(\weights^{(\iteridx+1)}, \offset^{(\iteridx+1)} ig) =  ig(\weights^{(\iteridx)}, \offset^{(\iteridx)} ig) - \lrate^{(\iteridx)} \vg^{(\iteridx)} 	ext{, for } \iteridx = 0,1,\ldots 	ext{,} \] with a step size η(t) > 0 and a subgradient g(t) ∈ ∂ f(w(t), b(t)) of the SVM objective function \eqref{eq:svm_rerm}. Inserting the SVM objective function \eqref{eq:svm_rerm} into the generic update yields the explicit update \[  egin{aligned} \weights^{(\iteridx+1)} &=  ig(1 - 2 \lrate^{(\iteridx)} egparam ig) \weights^{(\iteridx)} +  rac{\lrate^{(\iteridx)}}{\samplesize} \sum_{\sampleidx=1}^{\samplesize} \expcoeff^{(\iteridx)}_{\sampleidx} 	ruelabel^{(\sampleidx)}  eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)} 	ext{,} \offset^{(\iteridx+1)} &= \offset^{(\iteridx)} +  rac{\lrate^{(\iteridx)}}{\samplesize} \sum_{\sampleidx=1}^{\samplesize} \expcoeff^{(\iteridx)}_{\sampleidx} 	ruelabel^{(\sampleidx)} 	ext{,} \end{aligned} \] with expansion coefficients β(t)r ∈ [0, 1], for r = 1, …, m. The coefficient β(t)r is nonzero only for data points that satisfy y(r) ( (w(t))ᵀ x(r) + b(t) ) ≤ 1, i.e., data points on which the hinge loss is nonzero or non-differentiable at the current model parameters. Each iteration scales w(t) by the factor 1 - 2 η(t) α (the effect of the penalty term) and adds corrections y(r) x(r) only from these data points. In particular, for the initialization w(0) = 0, every iterate w(t) is a weighted sum Σr=1m βr y(r) x(r) of the feature vectors in the training set. The solution w of the SVM problem \eqref{eq:svm_rerm} admits the same expansion, with coefficients that are nonzero only for the data points satisfying the same condition y(r) ( wᵀ x(r) + b ) ≤ 1 (see the discussion of support vectors below). For a suitably diminishing step size, e.g., η(t) = 1/(t+1), the iterates (w(t), b(t)) converge to a solution of the SVM problem \eqref{eq:svm_rerm} . The solution of the SVM problem \eqref{eq:svm_rerm} has a clear geometric meaning when the training set is linearly separable, i.e., there is some choice for (w, b) such that sign(wᵀ x(r) + b) y(r)=1 for r=1, …, m. For a sufficiently small α, the solution of the SVM problem \eqref{eq:svm_rerm} determines the separating hyperplane that is farthest from the feature vectors in the training set (see Fig.~1). The distance between this hyperplane and the nearest feature vectors, which are precisely the support vectors discussed below, is referred to as the margin and is given by 1/w2. The margin of the learned hyperplane measures robustness against perturbations of the features of a data point. Perturbing a feature vector x by a vector δ changes the score wᵀx + b by wᵀδ, whose magnitude is at most w2 δ2 by the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality. A data point therefore stays correctly classified under every feature perturbation with δ2 < γ, as illustrated by the dashed circle around a support vector in Fig.~1. data points whose feature vectors lie farther from the decision boundary tolerate even larger perturbations: the tolerated radius equals the distance of the feature vector from the decision boundary, which is at least γ and equals γ precisely for the support vectors. Maximizing the margin maximizes this worst-case tolerated radius, so the maximum-margin classifier is the separating classifier that is robust against the largest feature perturbations. The same holds beyond the separable case: the SVM problem \eqref{eq:svm_rerm} is equivalent to minimizing the hinge loss under the worst case over bounded perturbations of the feature vectors . While the above discussion only applies when the SVM training set is linearly separable, every solution of the SVM problem \eqref{eq:svm_rerm} has a structure that holds in general. In particular, \widehat{\weights} = \sum_{\sampleidx=1}^{\samplesize} \expcoeff_{\sampleidx} 	ruelabel^{(\sampleidx)}  eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)} with expansion coefficients 0 ≤ βr ≤ 1/(2 α m) that are nonzero only for the feature vectors with 	ruelabel^{(\sampleidx)} (\widehat{\weights}^{	op}  eaturevec^{(\sampleidx)} + \widehat{\offset}) \leq 1. These feature vectors are referred to as support vectors since they entirely determine the SVM solution (w, b). Applying a perturbation to any feature vector that is not a support vector leaves w, b unchanged, provided the perturbation is small enough to preserve the strict inequality y (wᵀ x + b) > 1 (see Fig.~2). An overly large regularization parameter α makes the SVM underfit: the above expansion of w implies w2 ≤ ρ/(2α), with ρ the largest Euclidean norm of a feature vector in the training set, so w shrinks toward 0. Consider a training set with unequal class sizes. For sufficiently large α, the predictions approach the constant majority-class prediction sign(b) and every data point of the minority class is a misclassified support vector. The training set of Fig.~2 also has unequal class sizes, four data points labeled y = +1 and three labeled y = -1, but with the value α = 1/14 used there, the SVM misclassifies one data point that can be considered an outlier. Increasing the value of α to 50 for that training set shrinks w2, makes every prediction on the training set equal to +1, and misclassifies all three data points of the minority class. The basic SVM \eqref{eq:svm_rerm} learns a linear classifier on the feature space X = Rd. It can be generalized to a classification method for data points whose feature vectors lie in an arbitrary feature space X by first applying a feature transformation φ: X → X' with X' = Rd and then learning a linear classifier on the transformed feature vectors. By choosing φ (and d) appropriately, any given training set with distinct feature vectors can be made linearly separable in X', e.g., by mapping each distinct feature vector to its own coordinate axis . A principled construction of a feature transformation φ is via a kernel k: X × X → R. In this construction, the transformed feature space is, in general, not Rd but a Hilbert space H, which can be infinite-dimensional (see kernel method). The resulting method is then referred to as a kernel SVM .

The weather stations Krems an der Donau and Melk in the Wachau valley each record the minimum and the maximum temperature of every day. Predicting the maximum temperature y of a day at one station from its minimum temperature x is a learning task. Each weather station defines a separate learning task. Fig.~1 shows the two tasks side by side, each with a decision tree of depth two, the hypothesis learned from a training set of five labeled days of 2020. A learning task can be characterized by the probability distribution of its data points: at the two stations those distributions are similar. Transfer learning uses such similarity. It couples the learning tasks so that what is learned at one station improves the predictions at the other, and its effectiveness depends on how similar the probability distributions of the coupled tasks are . The coupling in Fig.~1 is implemented by adding a penalty to the objective function of the model training at each station. Let 1 and 2 be the hypotheses of the two stations and T a test set of feature values selected at random from the range [-10, 10] of minimum temperatures. Transfer learning then minimizes the sum of the two training errors plus α 1|T| Σx ∈ T ( 1(x) - 2(x) )2, the mean squared disagreement of the two hypotheses on the test features. For decision trees, minimizing over one tree while the other is fixed is a weighted fit of that tree to its own days together with the test features labeled by the other tree, and alternating the two fits solves the coupled problem. With five labeled days per station, the coupled trees agree on the test features (mean squared disagreement 32.2 → 0.1), and their average error on the held-out days of the two stations falls from 47.4 to 33.5. The coupling of two tasks extends to a collection of learning tasks, one per node i = 1, …, n of an undirected weighted graph G, the similarity graph. A weighted edge ii' with edge weight Ai,i' encodes the similarity between two learning tasks; a zero weight leaves them uncoupled. Each node i carries a local model, a local dataset, and the local hypothesis i learned from them. Minimizing the sum of the local training errors plus α times the generalized total variation \sum_{\edge{
